place.  The Ordinal Placement for each judge was based on Total Points awarded by that judge to the pairs, with the tiebreaker being Sporting Merit (2nd mark).  Final placement was determined by a Majority Placement rule.  Thus, if a pair were ranked first by a majority of the judges, that pair was placed first overall, and the process was repeated for each place.  If no absolute majority for a place existed, the tiebreakers were, in order: 1) Total Ordinals, 2) Total Points, 3) Compulsory Figure Points."]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #1"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/84759"} "Christen Christensen"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NOR.png"}] "NOR"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #2"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5003333"} "Bernard Fox"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #3"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5004277"} "Rudolf Kaler"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/AUT"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/AUT.png"}] "AUT"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #4"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5002009"} "Eugen Kirchhofer"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SUI"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SUI.png"}] "SUI"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #5"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5004796"} "Vladimír Koudelka"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/TCH"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/TCH.png"}] "TCH"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #6"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5003387"} "Marcel Nicaise"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/BEL"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/BEL.png"}] "BEL"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #7"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/82086"} "Mollie Phillips"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #8"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/96916"} "Melville Rogers"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/CAN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/CAN.png"}] "CAN"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #9"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/98169"} "Elemér Terták"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/HUN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/HUN.png"}] "HUN"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #10"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/1005253"} "Georges Torchon"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRA.png"}] "FRA"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #11"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5003485"} "Mario Verdi"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ITA.png"}] "ITA"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "In the first Figure Skating Championships after World War II, the European Championships in Davos 1947, the pair skating event was won by a relatively unknown Belgian couple, " [:a {:href "/athletes/81623"} "Micheline Lannoy"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/83906"} "Pierre Baugniet"] ", national champions since 1944. At the World Championship in Stockholm two weeks later, they were again on top of the rostrum, winning with a scant margin over a young and very promising US pair, siblings " [:a {:href "/athletes/81467"} "Karol"] " (15) and " [:a {:href "/athletes/87880"} "Peter Kennedy"] " (19), from Seattle, WA."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the first part of the Olympic season of 1948, “The Kennedy Kids” won their first national championships. The Belgian couple chose not to take part in the European Championship in Prague two weeks before the St. Moritz Games, and the Hungarians " [:a {:href "/athletes/81463"} "Andrea Kékesy"] "/" [:a {:href "/athletes/87973"} "Ede Király"] " won the title in a very convincing way."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The Olympic pairs competition became a close contest, which was hampered by being held during a snowstorm (it was outside). The Belgian couple were able to keep ahead of their rivals and secured Belgium’s first and, until 2022, only Winter Olympic Gold. At the Beijing 2022 Winter Olympics, more than 70 years after the 1948 Games, speed skater " [:a {:href "/athletes/127397"} "Bart Swings"] " won Belgium’s second Winter gold medal, in the " [:a {:href "/results/19020405"} "mass start event"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The newly crowned European Champions, Hungarians Kékesy/Király, won the silver medal ahead of the Canadian couple " [:a {:href "/athletes/81875"} "Suzi Morrow"] "/" [:a {:href "/athletes/85197"} "Wallace Diestelmeyer"] ". Karol Kennedy was hampered by a serious back injury, and the Kennedys ended in a disappointing 6th place."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Five sibling couples participated in St. Moritz, and they placed in a row from 5th to 9th. In 14th place, we find another family couple, wife and husband " [:a {:href "/athletes/81047"} "Denise"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/85646"} "Jacques Favart"] ", from Paris. About 20 years later, Jacques Favart had advanced to become President of the " [:a {:href "/organizations/2"} "International Skating Union"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Lannoy/Baugniet ended their career by winning the World title in Davos one week after the Olympics, and the medal distribution was the same as in St. Moritz. Karol Kennedy was still struggling with her back injury, and the Kennedys ended in 4th place."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Majority Placements"] [:th {} "Total Ordinals"] [:th {} "Total Points"] [:th {} "Reduced Points"] [:th {} "Judge #1"] [:th {} "Judge #2"] [:th {} "Judge #3"] [:th {} "Judge #4"] [:th {} "Judge #5"] [:th {} "Judge #6"] [:th {} "Judge #7"] [:th {} "Judge #8"] [:th {} "Judge #9"] [:th {} "Judge #10"] [:th {} "Judge #11"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/81623"} "Micheline Lannoy"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/83906"} "Pierre Baugniet"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/BEL"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/BEL.png"}] "BEL"]] [:td {} "7×1+"] [:td {} "17.5"] [:td {} "123.5"] [:td {} "11.227"] [:td {} "1.0 (1)"] [:td {} "2.0 (2)"] [:td {} "2.5 (=2)"] [:td {} "1.0 (1)"] [:td {} "1.5 (=1)"] [:td {} "1.0 (1)"] [:td {} "1.0 (1)"] [:td {} "1.0 (1)"] [:td {} "4.5 (=4)"] [:td {} "1.0 (1)"] [:td {} "1.0 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/81463"} "Andrea Kékesy"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/87973"} "Ede Király"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/HUN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.